L001308	
L001309	For both models, the system Hamiltonian can be programmed to simulate an ensemble of unitary time evolution operators $\Uh\left(t\right)$ (see Eq.~1 in the main text).
L001310	An intuitive way to measure SFF is to acquire the time evolution operators by quantum process tomography (QPT)~\cite{tomographyExp}.
L001311	However, the number of experimental runs needed to measure a time evolution operator using QPT scales exponentially with system size $L$.
L001312	Therefore, developing and implementing experimental protocols to efficiently measure SFF has recently attracted great interest~\cite{ZollerSFF2020, ZollerSFF2021, Dag:2022vqb}.
L001313	One of the most promising protocols is proposed in Ref.~\cite{ZollerSFF2021}, which is based on the randomized measurement toolbox~\cite{Elben_2022} and makes use of the statistical correlations of local random rotations available on the state-of-the-art quantum processors.
L001314	
L001315	\begin{figure}[t]
L001316	    \centering
L001317	    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_supp_pulse_sequence.pdf}
L001318	    \caption{
L001319	        {\bf Pulse sequence for probing the SFF and pSFF dynamics with a randomized measurement protocol.}
L001320	        We start with an initial product state $|0\rangle^{\otimes L}$. Before and after the unitary time evolutions $\Uh(t)$ of the Floquet or the Hamiltonian models, we apply random Clifford gates and their inverses, respectively. Finally, we measure all qubit simultaneously for $2^{L}$ occupation probabilities.
L001321	    }
L001322	    \label{fig: SFF_rand_meas_circuit}
L001323	\end{figure}
L001324	
L001325	The experimental protocol for measuring SFF using a randomized measurement toolbox is described as follows (also see Fig.~\ref{fig: SFF_rand_meas_circuit}).
L001326	We first initialize the system in the product state $|0\rangle^{\otimes L}$, and apply on-site high-fidelity random unitary gates $u_m$ that are independently drawn from the single-qubit Clifford gates.
L001327	
L001328	Secondly, we tune all qubits to be in resonance at the interaction frequency $\omega^{\mathrm{I}}$ and evolve the system with $\Uh(t)$ which is generated with either a time-periodic (the Floquet model) or a time-independent (the Hamiltonian model) Hamiltonian.
L001329	
L001330	Then, the adjoint local random unitaries $u_m^{\dagger}$ are applied on each qubit at its own idle frequency.
L001331	
L001332	Finally, we measure all qubits in the $z$-basis, and obtain a bit string of the form $\mathbf{s}=(s_1,\ldots,s_L)$ where $s_m \in \{0,1\}$ denotes the measurement result on site $m$.
L001333	For each realization of the unitary gates and the time evolution operator, the above procedures are repeated $3000$ times and a readout-error mitigation method~\cite{PhysRevLett.118.210504} is adopted to collect the probability of each bit string $\mathbf{s}$, denoted $P_{r}\left(\mathbf{s}\right)$.
L001334	
L001335	The SFF of a quantum many-body system then reads~\cite{ZollerSFF2021}
L001336	
L001337	\begin{equation}
L001338	
L001339	K\left(t\right)
L001340	= \frac{1}{R}
L001341	\sum_{r=1}^R
L001342	\sum_{\mathbf{s}}
L001343	P_{r}\left(\mathbf{s}\right)\left(-2\right)^{- |\mathbf{s}|} \; ,
L001344	\end{equation}
L001345	where $\left|\mathbf{s}\right|$ is defined as $\left|\mathbf{s}\right| := \sum_{m} s_{m}$ and $R$ is the realization number.
L001346	The estimated value for pSFF $K_A(t)$ can be calculated by projecting $P_{r}\left(\mathbf{s}\right)$ onto subsystem A for $P_{r}\left(\mathbf{s}_A\right)$
L001347	
L001348	\begin{equation}
L001349	
L001350	K_A\left(t\right)
L001351	= \frac{1}{R}
L001352	\sum_{r=1}^R
L001353	\sum_{\mathbf{s}_A}
L001354	P_{r}\left(\mathbf{s}_A\right)\left(-2\right)^{- |\mathbf{s}_A|} \; .
L001355	\end{equation}
L001356	
L001357	\section{Error mitigation and estimation of the plateau times}
L001358	
L001359	For both the Floquet and Hamiltonian models, the experimental plateau values of the SSF seem to be lower than the theoretical predictions, which is mainly due to the qubit decoherence. Consequently, in Fig.~2 and 3 of the main text, we provide numerical simulations with decoherence taken into account as more suitable references. These noisy numerics are acquired by describing the dynamics of the system with the Lindblad master equation given by
L001360	\begin{equation}
L001361	    \dot{\rho}\left(t\right) = -i \left[\hat{H}\left(t\right), \rho\left(t\right)\right]
L001362	    + \sum_{n} \sum_{m} \left(2 \hat{C}_{n, m}^{} \rho\left(t\right) \hat{C}_{n, m}^{\dagger} - \rho\left(t\right) \hat{C}_{n, m}^{\dagger} \hat{C}_{n, m}^{} - \hat{C}_{n, m}^{\dagger} \hat{C}_{n, m}^{} \rho\left(t\right)\right),
L001363	\end{equation}
L001364	where the jump operator $ \hat{C}_{n, m}^{}$ characterize the $n$-th kind of noise acting on the $m$-th qubit.
L001365	In our specific context, we include the amplitude damping noise and dephasing noise with jump operators given by
L001366	\begin{equation}
L001367	    \begin{split}
L001368	        C_{1, m} &= \sqrt{\frac{1}{T_{1, m}}} \hat{\sigma}_m^-, \\
L001369	        C_{2, m} &= \sqrt{\frac{2}{T_{2, m}}} \hat{\sigma}_m^+ \hat{\sigma}_m^-, \\
L001370	    \end{split}
L001371	\end{equation}
L001372	respectively. The values of qubit lifetimes $T_{1, m}$ and spin-echo times $T_{2, m}$ are provided in Table~\ref{tab:dev_para_hamiltonian}.
L001373	As shown in the main text, the noisy numerical results of the SFF, denoted by $K_{\mathrm{dec}}$ (and $K_{A, \mathrm{dec}}$), are in close agreement with the experimental results $K_{\mathrm{exp}}$ (and $K_{A, \mathrm{exp}}$).
L001374	
L001375	\begin{figure*}[htbp]
L001376	    \centering
L001377	    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig_supp_error_mitigation_floquet.pdf}
L001378	
L001379	    \caption{{\bf Error-mitigated SFF dynamics for the Floquet model.}
L001380	    Two different error mitigation schemes are implemented using the experimental SFF values (orange dots, both panels), which gives $K^{(1)}_{\mathrm{em}}(\tau)$ (gray dots, left panel) and $K^{(2)}_{\mathrm{em}}(\tau)$ (gray dots, right panel). The prediction of the SFF according to random matrix theory is shown as the solid lines.}
L001381	    \label{fig:error-mitigation}
L001382	\end{figure*}
L001383	
L001384	To better visualize the ramp-plateau and dip-ramp-plateau behaviors, we propose and implement two different kinds of error mitigation schemes. The first scheme is to rescale the experimental results according to
L001385	\begin{equation}
L001386	\begin{split}
L001387	    K^{(1)}_{\mathrm{em}}\left(t\right)
L001388	    &= \frac{K_{\mathrm{sim}} \left(t\right)}{K_{\mathrm{dec}} \left(t\right)}
L001389	    K_{\mathrm{exp}} \left(t\right) \; ,
L001390	
L001391	\end{split}
L001392	\end{equation}
L001393	
L001394	where $K_{\mathrm{sim}}$ is the numerical results without decoherence.
L001395	The second scheme is outlined in Ref.~\cite{ZollerSFF2021} for analysis of the Floquet model, with the assumption of errors only occurring through the depolarization channel.
L001396	The resulting formula is
L001397	\begin{equation}
L001398	\begin{split}
L001399	    K^{(2)}_{\mathrm{em}}(t) &= \frac{K_{\mathrm{dec}}\left(t\right)-\left(1-\alpha\left(t\right)\right)/N^2}{\alpha(t)} \; ,
L001400	
L001401	\end{split}
L001402	\end{equation}
L001403	
L001404	where $\alpha\left(t\right)=\sqrt{\left(N\mathrm{Tr}\left(\rho_{\mathrm{dec}}\left(t\right)^{2}\right) - 1\right)/\left(N-1\right)}$ is determined by numerical simulations with decoherence.
L001405	As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:error-mitigation}, two schemes give quite similar results for the Floquet model.
L001406	In the main text, we only provide the error-mitigated results using the first scheme, that is, $K^{(1)}_{\mathrm{em}}\left(t\right)$.
L001407	
L001408	To extract the plateau times of the SFF for the Hamiltonian model (Fig.~3 of the main text), we numerically fit the error-mitigated data $K^{(1)}_{\mathrm{em}} \left(t\right)$ using the formula $f\left(t; \; \mu, \thei\right) = \mu K_{\mathrm{GOE}}\left(t; \; \thei\right)$, where $\mu$ and $\thei$ are two fitting parameters.
L001409	In each inset of Fig.~3(c)-(f) of the main text, we plot $K^{\prime}\left(t\right) = \mu^{-1} N K^{(1)}_{\mathrm{em}} \left(t\right)$ together with the corresponding fitting curve $N K_{\mathrm{GOE}}\left(t; \; \thei\right)$.
L001410	
L001411	\section{Experimental results of the pSFF for other system sizes}
L001412	
L001413	Here, in Fig.~\ref{Fig_PSFF1} and \ref{Fig_PSFF2}, we provide additional experimental results of the pSFF data $K_{A}$ together with the corresponding purity $\tilde{P}_{B}$ ($L=2$, $3$, and $4$), which are not shown in the main text.
L001414	
L001415	Importantly, we observe that the plateau time of pSFF is representative and roughly independent of the subsystem size $L_A$, which is also equal to the plateau time of the whole system, both in the Floquet and the Hamiltonian models.
L001416	
